\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/05/059731f0cac61f19382172192c6a9f7f366ecb758767797f9bc8311faa3865af.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07749363-23c7-503e-a2b3-393678d57dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256846} {:answer/id #uuid "24c0e96f-3c12-53d3-a715-c6e7e9906c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257090} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0ce482-ffc1-5d07-8079-2bd0daaa2c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257183} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecdb6ee-cacb-5adb-943a-1f4974cf994d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257911} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b9c622-d025-50e5-99b8-4c7b998a1654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07749363-23c7-503e-a2b3-393678d57dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256846} :answer-field/id #uuid "a309116f-e876-54bf-836d-2975cfa79bb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e666f143-8faf-54d3-a90e-9cb72a05e2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125493" :question/problem "![](images/61/61147f12b8c73562b7153740066ef8c7209d3dc84612d3667630bc9460918c03.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a6517f5b42ed2ffd18522ce13d817c3094245c650c41d0f456116914c818436.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/38/38868b69ae1836844a279e141d83b886f386c87edeedbb1e2067ffd18d7f1af3.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-